  "kw": [],
  "cause": "**By Saturday the explanation had changed twice, and the second version is worse than the first.** On Friday afternoon a contractor, **L3Harris**, was replacing copper cabling with fibre-optic cable in a decades-old aviation telecoms network — the copper was so old it was being discontinued — and at a remote site it installed a **router**. According to **Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy**, that router began *\"flooding\"* the other systems in the air traffic control network, sending about **1,000 connection attempts every four to five seconds**. **Bryan Bedford**, the FAA administrator, said **17 of the 22 air route traffic control centres** in the United States experienced instability in voice and ground communications. At **15:54 EST** every departing flight in the continental US was stopped; the order held for more than four hours, the longest such stoppage since **11 September 2001**. **The most important number in this story is forty-five minutes, and what happened during it.** It took the FAA that long to find where the problem was, Bedford admitted, *because maintenance is logged manually on paper* — so staff had to *\"pore through\"* physical documents *\"to figure out who's working on what.\"* The agency that regulates a continent's airspace could not answer, electronically, which contractor was working on which component. Duffy said the government is now trying to establish whether the fault was *\"incompetence\"* on the part of the company doing the upgrades or faulty equipment, and that there would be a *\"re-evaluation\"* of allowing upgrades during the day; **L3Harris** said it had completed *\"more than 70%\"* of its modernisation work before the shutdown, takes the impact *\"seriously\"*, and is supporting the investigation. **Bedford**: *\"zero evidence of anything nefarious and zero evidence of anything AI related.\"* More than **15,000** flights were delayed or cancelled on Friday and a further **5,900 cancelled** and **7,000 delayed** on Saturday, with about **1.5 million** passengers affected; the FAA says the schedule should be normal by the end of Sunday. **Trump** has requested **\\\\$30.1bn**; Duffy puts the real figure at **\\\\$50bn**; both chambers of Congress are held by the president's party. Aviation analyst **Miles O'Brien** called the system *\"a house that's about to be condemned.\"*",
  "bullets": [
   "**The failure mode was not age, it was scheduling — and the fix being debated is money, which is the wrong instrument** — a working upgrade, performed by a contractor, during operating hours, on a network with no electronic change log; the direct effect is that the same fault can recur next week at a different site, because nothing about the router has been corrected; the compound consequence is that \\\\$30.1bn or \\\\$50bn of capital spending buys new equipment while leaving untouched the two things that actually failed — the **change-management window** and the **asset register** — so the upgrade will be procured, delivered, and then operated with the same paper log, and the first test of the new system will be a maintenance error it cannot trace",
   "**A forty-five-minute fault localisation is a structural property of the network, not an accident of staffing** — maintenance logged by hand, in a system whose failure halts a continent; the direct effect is that detection time is set by how many people are reading paper at that moment; the compound consequence is that every future incident begins with the same blind interval, and the interval is exactly when the wrong decision gets made — the FAA grounded everything because it could not see what was happening, which means the *scale* of the stoppage is a function of the *opacity* of the records, and any operator with better records would have grounded fewer planes",
   "**When the cause is attributed to a contractor's \"incompetence\" before the investigation closes, the regulator has already assigned the blame it will otherwise have to carry** — Duffy named a company, a percentage of work completed, and a fault class within a day, while the root cause remains *\"under way\"*; the direct effect is that public attention moves from the funding decision to the vendor's competence; the compound consequence is that the real accountability question — who authorised live-system maintenance during peak operations, on a network with no electronic log — goes unasked in the only forum where it could be answered, and a regulator that blames its supplier has no reason to change its own approval process",
   "**The traffic and the risk are inversely distributed: the places with the oldest equipment carry the most flights** — 17 of 22 centres degraded simultaneously, in a system already handling **1.5 million** passengers a day, eleven days before **Thanksgiving**; the direct effect is that the pre-holiday load test will run on a network that has just demonstrated a single point of failure; the compound consequence is that airlines, having absorbed two days of cancellations with no compensable cause, will price the risk into fares and schedules rather than into the infrastructure — and the passenger pays for the unrepaired system twice, once in the ticket and once in the terminal",
   "**\"Zero evidence of anything AI related\" is the sentence that will age worst** — stated twice, by two officials, in a system where a flood of automated connection attempts from one router produced a continental ground stop; the direct effect is that the reassurance is true and irrelevant, because the harm did not require intelligence, only automation; the compound consequence is that the public debate is now framed around malicious AI and cyber-attack — the two causes ruled out — while the cause in evidence, unlogged maintenance on undocumented infrastructure, receives no scrutiny at all, and the next incident will again be classified as unforeseeable because nobody is looking at the category it belongs to"
  ],
  "verse": "Bearing the responsibility is always the first thing you have to do, but the by far largest number amongst you human beings of Earth, you push off the bearing and exercising of the responsibility onto your imaginary godheads, tin gods, angels, saints and to idolised human beings as well as onto your next ones or other fellow human beings, because on the one hand you see the responsibility as a burden with which you do not want to encumber yourselves and, on the other hand, because the bearing of the responsibility is, out of cowardice, too much for you, or because you think that you are not responsible yourselves.",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 28, Verse 38",
  "hinge": "§28:38 describes the exact manoeuvre performed this week: responsibility pushed onto *\"next ones or other fellow human beings\"* — first onto the age of the equipment, then onto a router, then onto a contractor's competence, and finally onto Congress for not appropriating. Each transfer is individually plausible and collectively a refusal. The hinge is small and cheap: **require an electronic maintenance log**. Every change to a system that touches flight safety should be entered in a machine-readable register — who, what, where, when, rollback plan — visible to the FAA's operations centre in real time, with a rule that no work proceeds on a live safety system without an entry. That single requirement converts the forty-five-minute blind interval into a lookup, and it costs a fraction of the \\\\$30.1bn. **Then** spend the money, in the order the register reveals. And publish the incident report — the router's identity, the change ticket, the detection timeline, the decision log — because a regulator that investigated in public would not need to announce that it takes the impact seriously. Four pages of procedure would have kept 1.5 million people moving. The alternative being offered is a \\\\$50bn purchase order written by the same process that produced the outage."
